Phase 1: Image Refinement + Tesseract/EasyOCR Baseline

Unify your preprocessing into a single function (grayscale, threshold, denoise, deskew).
Call that on every image before you hand it to Tesseract or EasyOCR.

Log confidences (via image_to_data) so you know which characters/words are still tripping
 the engine.

Ensemble outputs: for each word position, pick the highest-confidence prediction
 across engines.

Goal: nail down a stable, reproducible pipeline that gets you ≥ 90% token
accuracy.

Phase 2: Custom Model Training

Reuse the same refinements in your Dataset pipeline. That way your CRNN/TrOCR
sees exactly what your baseline did and learns from the cleaned inputs.

Augment with small variations on your refinements (e.g. slightly different
thresholds, random rotations of ±1°) so your model generalizes.

Integrate a light LM during decoding:

If you use CTC + beam search, plug in a bigram/trigram LM
(e.g. “after for the token i is 45% likely”) to bias the decoder toward valid code tokens.

Or after greedy decoding, run a quick n-gram corrector for common mistakes
(f0r→for, missing colons, etc.).

Goal: push your token accuracy past 95% on held-out scans before you even touch an LLM.


Phase 3: LLM & Statistical Polishing

Raw text → LLM prompt

“Here’s a Python snippet with no indentation. Please add proper indentation and
 fix any minor typos—don’t change the logic.”

Post-LLM validation

Parse with ast.parse to catch syntax errors.

Fallback to a second pass or highlight failures for manual review.

Statistical checks

Maintain bigram counts from your own codebase (or from a corpus of Python code)
to catch improbable token sequences.

E.g., if your model outputs for i in range( you know a ) or : must follow.

Why this will max out accuracy
You’re squeezing every drop from classic OCR engines first.

Your custom model is trained on the same cleaned inputs, so there’s no domain mismatch.

Language priors (n-grams or transformer prompts) catch the last 1–2% of weird symbol errors.

Automated validation with AST/linter prevents you from shipping broken code.

